        'Annual electricity use has stayed roughly flat or fallen slightly since 2015, thanks to more efficient appliances and high prices after 2021. The problem is when and where. About 1.3 million plug-in cars (early 2024) and over 620,000 extra heat pumps since 2015 draw a lot of power at the same moments.\n\nNetbeheer Nederland estimates that the average power per home at peak rose from about 1.5 to about 5 kW. That means far fewer homes fit on one transformer.',
        'On the generation side, solar capacity grew by a factor of 28 since 2015, to almost 29 GW, and in 2024 more than half (54%) of electricity came from renewable sources for the first time. On sunny afternoons the Netherlands sometimes generates more than it uses, and power flows “the wrong way”: from neighbourhoods to the high-voltage grid.\n\nMany solar parks also sit where the grid has always been thin, in the countryside.',
        'What is happening: the National Grid Congestion Action Programme (LAN), since late 2022, brings together the national government, the grid operators, the ACM and the provinces and municipalities: build faster, use the grid better, share more smartly. The investment plans work with scenarios for 2030 to 2050, such as the Integrated Infrastructure Outlook 2030–2050 (II3050).\n\nYet demand grows faster than building can keep up: an extension of the high-voltage grid easily takes six to ten years.',
